### 1.2.1-1.2.2 From the ordinary derivative to the gradient

**Where we are.** Section 1.2 asks one question: how do we differentiate a function of *three* variables? It is answered in three steps: the ordinary derivative $df/dx$; its generalization to a function of three variables, the gradient $\nabla T = (\partial T/\partial x)\hat{\mathbf{x}} + (\partial T/\partial y)\hat{\mathbf{y}} + (\partial T/\partial z)\hat{\mathbf{z}}$; and then the vector operator $\nabla$ (del), which produces the gradient and, as we shall see, the divergence and the curl as well. The operator $\nabla$ itself is defined in the next unit, after the gradient has earned its meaning.

### The ordinary derivative is a proportionality factor

For a function of one variable, $f(x)$, the derivative tells us how rapidly $f$ varies when the argument changes by a tiny amount $dx$. Increment $x$ by the infinitesimal $dx$ and $f$ changes by $df$; the derivative is exactly the factor relating the response to the cause:

> [!note] Key equation · Ordinary derivative as a proportionality factor (Eq. 1.33)
>
> $$
> df = \left(\frac{df}{dx}\right) dx
> $$
>
> **Symbols:** $dx$ → infinitesimal change in the argument $x$; $df$ → resulting change in $f$; $df/dx$ → the ordinary derivative, the proportionality factor (slope)
>
> **Assumptions:** f is differentiable in one variable; $dx$ is an infinitesimal increment.
>
> **Meaning:** An infinitesimal increment $dx$ of the argument produces the change $df$, with the derivative as the proportionality factor; geometrically $df/dx$ is the slope of $f$ versus $x$.
>
> *Source: PDF page 32.*

Geometrically, $df/dx$ is the **slope** of the graph of $f$ versus $x$. The two panels of Fig. 1.17 show the extremes: the gently undulating curve in (a) changes very little as $x$ advances, so its slope is small; the curve in (b) rises steeply away from $x = 0$, so its derivative is large there.

> [!example] Figure · PDF page 32
>
> ![[../Assets/p0032-snapshot-171c07f33dfc5df0.png|720]]
>
> Figure 1.17: (a) f varies slowly with x, so df/dx is small; (b) f rises rapidly away from x = 0, so the derivative is large.
>
> *Source: PDF viewer page 32.*

### Three variables: many directions, one theorem

Take the temperature $T(x,y,z)$ in a room. The question of how fast $T$ varies now has infinitely many answers, one for each direction you might walk: straight up, the temperature probably climbs quickly; horizontally, it may barely change. A single number can no longer summarize the derivative.

The rescue is a theorem on partial derivatives. Write $\partial T/\partial x$ for the derivative of $T$ with respect to $x$ while $y$ and $z$ are held fixed: it is the rate at which $T$ changes per unit step in the $x$ direction, evaluated at the point $(x,y,z)$. The total differential $dT$, unlike $\partial T$, collects the changes produced by all three coordinate increments at once.

Why should three one-variable rates summarize infinitely many directions? For infinitesimal increments, $T(x+dx, y+dy, z+dz) - T(x,y,z)$ is linear in $dx$, $dy$ and $dz$ to first order: each coordinate contributes its own rate with the other two held fixed, all rates evaluated at the starting point $(x,y,z)$, while products of two increments are second order and negligible. Since any small step has some $dx$, $dy$ and $dz$, these three rates cover every direction — and setting $dy = dz = 0$ reduces the statement to the one-variable result. Changing all three variables at once by the infinitesimal amounts $dx$, $dy$ and $dz$ therefore changes $T$ by the sum of the separate changes:

> [!note] Key equation · Total differential from partial derivatives (Eq. 1.34)
>
> $$
> dT = \left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial x}\right) dx + \left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial y}\right) dy + \left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial z}\right) dz


