```
Hmm, careful: the original filter dropped *checkpoints of the replaced roles only* (`latest.has(role)`), keeping other checkpoints. With prune, checkpoints superseded by a completed receipt for the same group are dropped; a checkpoint for a role with no completed receipt is kept — but is it still relevant? It's the same source/model, so it carries finished batches → useful for resume ✓ kept. Multiple checkpoints for the same group? Keep newest only (newest === receipt check ✓ others dropped). Fine.

Hmm: does dropping old checkpoints break resume? A checkpoint's purpose: the author's next submission reuses its finished batches. `runReviewPass` reads `existingReviews` = section.learnQuality.reviews and builds the cache from batches of receipts matching sourceHash+model ✓. With pruning: newest checkpoint per role+hash kept ✓ (that's what resume needs). But careful: contentHash changes when the lesson changes; a checkpoint for an older contentHash of the same role with no completed receipt → is it newest for its group? If a newer checkpoint exists for a different contentHash, then the older one is dropped (not newest, not newestCompleted) — but its batches belong to the *old* content → those batches' packets hashed differently → unusable anyway? Hmm: batches are keyed by packet hash (independent of contentHash) — packets include the lesson payload, so a lesson edit changes the packet key → the old batch wouldn't hit the cache anyway. But the *source window* packets are unaffected by lesson content changes... wait, source packets' payload includes `passages` (topic markdown) → changes with the lesson ✓; also `checklist`. So mostly invalidated. Except the source packet for pages whose topics didn't change? The payload includes all topics touching those pages, hashed per packet → a change elsewhere may not affect this packet → its cached batch still applies! So dropping an older checkpoint for a different contentHash could lose reusable packet work.

Hmm. But is that a *correctness* problem? No — worst case a packet is re-run (cost), not a wrong approval. And the load-time benefit (fewer stale receipts in the note) is the point of Step 11. Since the user's complaint is stale artifacts, pruning is intended. Note rule (2)/(4) keep only newest per group; older-group checkpoints are dropped when a newer group exists. Hmm, that could drop useful resume checkpoints after an edit + interruption... Trade-off accepted by the plan ("remove stale load artifacts").

Hmm wait, actually let me re-read rule (2): "keep the newest completed receipt per (role, contentHash)". It says nothing about dropping *checkpoints* of other groups. Rule (3) drops superseded checkpoints only. So maybe the intent is: keep checkpoints unless superseded (regardless of other groups), and drop only duplicate completed receipts and stale (source/model) ones.

That's more conservative and preserves resume capability. So:

```ts
  return currentOnly.filter((receipt) => {
    const key = groupOf(receipt);
    if (isCheckpoint(receipt)) return !newestCompleted.has(key);     // (3) superseded checkpoints go
    return receipt === newest.get(key) || receipt === newestCompleted.get(key) || Boolean(receipt.failure);
  });
```
i.e. keep: the newest of the group, the newest completed of the group, and any failure receipt (unfinished evidence — cheap, informative, and the gate may consume it). Drop: superseded checkpoints, older duplicate completed receipts, and older duplicate failures.

Hmm, "any failure receipt" would keep multiple old failures of the same group (if not newest and not completed). E.g. two old provider failures for the same group → keep newest (=== newest ✓) and the older one → keep too (failure) → clutter. Better: keep failures only if they are the newest of their group ✓ (they'd be the gate consumer). Older failures are superseded.

Final filter:
```ts
  return currentOnly.filter((receipt) => {
    const key = groupOf(receipt);
    const supersededCheckpoint = isCheckpoint(receipt) && newestCompleted.has(key);   // (3)
    return !supersededCheckpoint && (receipt === newest.get(key) || receipt === newestCompleted.get(key));   // (2) + (4)
  });
```
Cases: completed(older) + completed(newer) → newest kept ✓. completed(older, same group) + failed(newer) → newest(failed) ✓ + newestCompleted(older) ✓ both kept. failure(older) + completed(newer) → newest=completed ✓ + newestCompleted=completed (same) → the older failure dropped ✓ good. checkpoint alone → isCheckpoint && !completed → kept via newest ✓.

Good: minimal, keeps resume checkpoints (unless superseded), keeps gate consumers, drops stale-source/model and duplicate historical receipts. Matches rules (1)-(4) closely. I'll document the trade in the comment.

